## Encryption in transit for snapshots

GCP has no "in transit" switch on a snapshot. Google encrypts traffic between VMs, disks, and snapshot storage by default. So the requirement can be met in one of three ways, depending on what the auditor means:

| Option | What it is | Cost | Complexity | Downtime |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Evidence only** | Document that Google encrypts this traffic by default, with screenshots and the snapshot describe output | none | low | none |
| **B. CMEK on snapshots** | Cloud KMS key; snapshots encrypted with your key | small KMS cost per key and operation | medium | none for new disks |
| **C. CMEK on disks** | Re-create disks with a KMS key so schedules inherit it | KMS cost plus rebuild effort | high | ⚠️ yes, for the standalone VMs |

I haven't verified B and C against the current GCP docs, so I won't give you commands for either yet. As far as I know, existing disk encryption can't be changed in place.

**Recommendation:** start with A. The wording says "encryption in transit", which Google provides by default, and A costs nothing. Move to B only if the auditor specifically asks for customer-managed keys.

